m68k backend: btst for a one-bit field in memory tested the wrong byte.

*tst_bftst_mem, *tst_bftst_mem1 and *tst_bftst_mem2 (SImode, HImode and
QImode memory) emit btst for a one-bit field with a constant position. They
used bit number 7 - POS on the operand's first byte. POS counts from the most
significant bit of the whole operand (0..31 for SImode), so for any bit in the
low 24 bits of a long the number went below 0 ("btst #-23,(a3)" for bit 1),
and btst on memory only uses the low three bits of the number: the bit of
the FIRST byte was tested instead of the right one. Code like

    unsigned info[256];  ...  if (info[i] & 2) ...
    if (*req & MEMF_CLEAR) ...

took the wrong branch at -O1 and up (68020+, bitfield patterns). Found in
ApolloOS R10, 2026-11-16: Regina's upper-case table stayed empty, exec's
stdAllocReverse never cleared MEMF_CLEAR memory, reqtools tested a wrong
flag. Not the bbb pass (-fbbb=- does not change it).

The fix tests bit 7 - POS % 8 of byte POS / 8, as the btst pattern for an
offsettable SImode operand above already does. The operand's constraints
(o, Q, U) never have an auto-increment address, so adjust_address is valid.
ApolloOS R10 (7662 C files) compiled with and without the fix: the
assembly differs only in the four miscompiled btst instructions, which
become "btst #1,(3,a5)", "btst #0,(25,sp)" etc. Upstream GCC has a QImode
operand here; the wider modes are this fork's.

diff --git a/gcc/config/m68k/m68k.md b/gcc/config/m68k/m68k.md
index f2f31d6..5970de8 100644
--- a/gcc/config/m68k/m68k.md
+++ b/gcc/config/m68k/m68k.md
@@ -6163,9 +6163,17 @@
   if (operands[1] == const1_rtx
       && GET_CODE (operands[2]) == CONST_INT)
     {
-      int width = GET_CODE (operands[0]) == REG ? 31 : 7;
+      /* A one-bit field in memory: btst on the byte that holds the bit.
+	 The field position counts from the most significant bit of the
+	 operand, so the bit is in byte POS / 8 and has number
+	 7 - POS % 8 there.  Using 7 - POS on the first byte, as before,
+	 gave bit numbers below 0 for POS > 7 (e.g. "btst #-23,(a0)"), and
+	 btst on memory only uses the low three bits of the number: it
+	 tested a bit of the wrong byte.  */
+      operands[0] = adjust_address (operands[0], QImode,
+				    INTVAL (operands[2]) / 8);
       return output_btst (operands,
-			  GEN_INT (width - INTVAL (operands[2])),
+			  GEN_INT (7 - INTVAL (operands[2]) % 8),
 			  operands[0], insn, 1000);
